  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000121149,
      "SummaryText": "The City of Mount Vernon's Wastewater Treatment Plant uses  Chlorine as an effective oxidizer to kill  pathogenic organisms in water. Chlorine reduces the risks of diseases to our  community and also to the  surrounding environment.   This chlorine product is regulated by federal and  state EPA to  minimize possible risks to the public.  To ensure that these guidelines are being meant, the following actions have taken place. \n\nCity of Mount Vernon Wastewater Department Policy:  Safety to the Public and our employees is our primary concern. As a part of the public service sector, we are committed in protecting the public and the environment.  Our programs include an aggressive preventive maintenance program for equipment and also an extensive safety training program. The department of Wastewater has coordinated with local emergency response services to efficiently react to any emergences. \n\nThe Description of facility:  In the treatment of Wastewater for the City of Mount Vernon and  surrounding area.  Part of treatment process requires the facility to eliminate all disease-causing bacteria, or (pathogens) through a disinfection process.  This process uses Chlorine to kill bacteria and other pathogens before they enter into the Kokosing river. \n\nIn accordance with the federal and state EPA guidelines, an analysis of the impact distance in the case of an accidental chlorine release of the connected container was performed.  This analysis was made using the EPA methods and guidance that were available. Stored chlorine not connected to the system, was determined not to be at reasonable risk and therefore was not included the model.  This model includes a release of chlorine, failure of all safety systems, and unfavorable weather conditions.  This model predicts the area which could be affected by as little as 1/10 of concentration of Chlorine gas, this would be considered immediately dangerous to public health . \n\nIn this model, a circle was generated on a map which sho"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000121149,
      "SummaryText": "ws the effected aeras of a chorine failure, this circle or radius would effect  approximately 1.3 miles.\nIn case of department emergences, our personnel would work with the local fire department responders and the Local Emergency Planning Committee.  This program has an annual safety training program for all operators here at the treatment facility. Through this program the department would be in constant coordination with the local fire department for any possible emergency scenarios to the community.  \n \nAs part of the requirements of this federal and state EPA program, we have carefully reviewed our accident history for the last 5 years, and found that we have operated without any reportable chlorine related incidents.  \n\nToo efficiently operate our treatment system, the department would need to  have 10,000 pounds of chlorine on the premises at any time.  We will remain in compliance with all federal, state and local regulatory agencies as part of our continuing efforts to provide quality services to our community.\n\nWe are continually looking for new methods to improve our safety program here at the City of Mount Vernon's Wastewater Treatment.  To insure safety, we have instituted new procedures to perform  safety reviews of all mechanical equipment and any changes to the treatment process."
    }
